    <section id="opening" aria-labelledby="opening-heading">
      <p class="sample__part">Opening</p>
      <h2 id="opening-heading">The record is the game board</h2>

      <p class="sample__lead">A criminal jury trial is not a television story or a public vote.</p>

      <p>It is a controlled legal process. The prosecution carries a burden set by law. The judge decides which evidence the jury may consider and explains the law the jury must apply. Lawyers argue for competing conclusions, but their questions and arguments are not evidence. Jurors are supposed to decide the case from the admitted record and the court's instructions, not from headlines, comments, rumors, or their own investigation.</p>

      <p>That sounds simple until a real trial begins. A witness may sound confident and still be mistaken. A scientific result may be important but narrower than a headline suggests. An objection may keep the jury from hearing something that people outside the courtroom already know. A judge may instruct jurors to use an item for one limited purpose and not another. Two people can watch the same testimony and disagree because they are answering different questions.</p>

      <p>The useful way to follow a trial is therefore not to ask, "Which side do I like?" It is to keep three columns:</p>

      <ul class="sample__columns">
        <li><strong>What the record shows:</strong> admitted testimony, exhibits, stipulations, and the judge's instructions.</li>
        <li><strong>What a side argues:</strong> the meaning the prosecution or defense asks jurors to draw from that record.</li>
        <li><strong>What remains unknown:</strong> gaps, disputed inferences, excluded material, and questions the public record cannot answer.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>This discipline does not tell you who will win. It does something more useful: it stops an allegation from becoming a fact merely because it was repeated, and it stops a dramatic fact from carrying more weight than the law allows.</p>

      <p>The first place to practice that discipline is jury selection. Before opening statements, a court must turn a group of summoned citizens into a panel that can decide the case fairly. That process is called <em>voir dire</em>. It is not a reliable personality test, and a spectator cannot know a juror's private thoughts. But the questions, answers, rulings, and strikes can show what risks the court and lawyers are trying to manage.</p>
    </section>

