    <section id="selection" aria-labelledby="selection-heading">
      <p class="sample__part">Sample lesson</p>
      <h2 id="selection-heading">How jury selection works</h2>

      <h3>What voir dire is</h3>

      <p><em>Voir dire</em> is the process of questioning prospective jurors to determine whether they can serve fairly and impartially. Procedures differ among federal and state courts and even among judges. The judge may lead most of the questioning, permit lawyers to ask questions, use written questionnaires, or question an individual privately when the subject is sensitive.</p>

      <p>A typical sequence includes a summons, initial eligibility or hardship questions, questions about knowledge and possible bias, rulings on requests to remove jurors, selection of jurors and alternates, and an oath. That sequence is a map, not a universal script. The controlling law and the judge's directions in the actual courtroom matter.</p>

      <h3>What the questions are testing</h3>

      <p>Questions may explore prior knowledge of the case, relationships with a participant, experiences related to the subject matter, strong views about law enforcement or criminal accusations, ability to follow the burden of proof, and practical ability to serve. Having an experience or opinion does not automatically disqualify someone. The court usually needs to decide whether the person can set it aside and follow the law and evidence.</p>

      <p>For an outside observer, the safest unit of analysis is an observable statement. "Juror 14 said she would automatically believe a police officer" is an observable report if that is what the juror said. "Juror 14 is a prosecution juror" is a prediction. The first can be checked against the record; the second may be wrong.</p>

      <h3>Two different kinds of removal</h3>

      <p>A <strong>challenge for cause</strong> asks the court to remove a prospective juror for a stated reason, such as demonstrated bias or another legal inability to serve. The judge decides. The number of valid for-cause removals is not capped in the way peremptory challenges are, but a lawyer does not receive one merely by attaching the word "bias" to a request. The record and the governing standard matter.</p>

      <p>A <strong>peremptory challenge</strong> generally permits a side to remove a limited number of prospective jurors without proving the same kind of cause. It is not law-free. Constitutional doctrine, including <em>Batson v. Kentucky</em> and later cases, forbids purposeful discrimination in jury selection on protected grounds. Procedures, allocations, and additional protections vary by jurisdiction.</p>

      <p>Do not infer a lawyer's private motive from a strike unless the record supports it. You can report who was struck, which side exercised the strike, whether an objection was raised, the explanation placed on the record, and the judge's ruling. Anything beyond that should be labeled as argument or inference.</p>

      <h3>Three mistakes to avoid</h3>

      <p><strong>First, do not treat a voir-dire question as evidence.</strong> A lawyer may ask whether a juror could remain fair if the evidence showed a certain fact. The question helps test the juror; it does not establish that the fact is true or that the lawyer will prove it. Keep hypothetical questions out of your evidence column.</p>

      <p><strong>Second, do not turn one answer into a personality diagnosis.</strong> A careful observer can record the answer and the follow-up. A spectator usually cannot know whether a pause means confusion, caution, discomfort, or something else. Demographics and body language are not shortcuts to a future vote.</p>

      <p><strong>Third, do not declare that a side "won the jury" before the trial starts.</strong> The seated panel must still hear evidence, receive instructions, and deliberate. Jury selection can reveal concerns and strategies, but it cannot provide a verdict in advance. Even when a court removes a prospective juror, the ruling addresses that person's ability to serve under the governing standard; it does not prove the prosecution or defense theory.</p>

      <p>These limits make coverage more accurate. They also make it more interesting, because the real question becomes visible: how does the legal system try to build an impartial decision-maker from ordinary people who arrive with experiences, opinions, and incomplete information?</p>

      <h3>The judge's role</h3>

      <p>The judge manages questioning, rules on challenges, applies the governing law, and determines whether the final panel can serve. A ruling that a juror may remain does not certify that the juror has no opinions. It means the court did not find a legal basis for removal under the applicable record and standard at that point.</p>

      <h3>What a careful trial watcher records</h3>

      <div class="sample__watch" role="note" aria-labelledby="watch-heading">
        <h3 id="watch-heading">Watch for this</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Who asks the question: judge, prosecution, or defense?</li>
          <li>What exact answer creates concern?</li>
          <li>Does follow-up questioning soften, deepen, or clarify the concern?</li>
          <li>Is removal requested for cause or made with a peremptory challenge?</li>
          <li>What does the judge actually rule?</li>
          <li>Which part is a fact from the proceeding, and which part is your prediction?</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="exercise" aria-labelledby="exercise-heading">
      <p class="sample__part">Practical exercise</p>
      <h2 id="exercise-heading">Read the answers without reading minds</h2>

      <p>Imagine a fictional robbery trial. The prosecution expects testimony from two police officers and a store employee. The defense says the identification is mistaken. During jury selection, three prospective jurors give these answers:</p>

      <div class="sample__scenario">
        <p><strong>Prospective Juror A:</strong> "My brother is a police officer. I think officers usually try to tell the truth, but I understand they can make mistakes. I would judge each witness by the same instructions."</p>
        <p><strong>Prospective Juror B:</strong> "I was robbed ten years ago. The person was never caught. I still get angry thinking about it. I do not know whether I could listen to this case without thinking about what happened to me."</p>
        <p><strong>Prospective Juror C:</strong> "I saw a short video about this case online. It said the defendant confessed. I have not seen any court evidence, and I can decide only from what is admitted here."</p>
      </div>

      <h3>Your task</h3>

      <ol>
        <li>Write one observable fact for each prospective juror. Use only the words given above.</li>
        <li>Write one question that needs follow-up for each person.</li>
        <li>Mark each of these statements <strong>record</strong>, <strong>inference</strong>, or <strong>unknown</strong>:
          <ol type="a">
            <li>Juror A has a close relative in law enforcement.</li>
            <li>Juror A will vote guilty.</li>
            <li>Juror B expressed doubt about remaining impartial.</li>
            <li>Juror B must legally be removed.</li>
            <li>Juror C encountered an out-of-court claim about a confession.</li>
            <li>The online confession claim is true.</li>
          </ol>
        </li>
        <li>Identify the decision-maker for a challenge for cause.</li>
        <li>Write one sentence a careful reporter could publish without predicting any juror's vote.</li>
      </ol>

      <p>Pause before reading the answer key. The goal is not to guess which side likes each juror. The goal is to separate what happened in court from what someone thinks it means.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="answer-key" aria-labelledby="answer-heading">
      <p class="sample__part">Answer key</p>
      <h2 id="answer-heading">A disciplined read</h2>

      <h3>1. Observable facts</h3>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>A:</strong> A said a brother is a police officer, officers usually try to tell the truth, officers can make mistakes, and each witness should be judged under the same instructions.</li>
        <li><strong>B:</strong> B described being robbed, continuing anger, and uncertainty about separating that experience from the fictional case.</li>
        <li><strong>C:</strong> C saw an online claim that the defendant confessed and said the decision should rest only on admitted evidence.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>2. Useful follow-up</h3>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>A:</strong> "If an officer and another witness gave conflicting accounts, could you evaluate both without automatically favoring the officer?"</li>
        <li><strong>B:</strong> "What part of your experience makes you unsure, and do you believe you could set it aside if the judge instructed you to decide only this case?"</li>
        <li><strong>C:</strong> "Have you formed an opinion about whether the online claim is accurate, and could you disregard it completely unless admissible evidence proves it?"</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>3. Record, inference, or unknown</h3>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>A has a close relative in law enforcement - record.</strong> The stated family relationship is observable.</li>
        <li><strong>A will vote guilty - inference.</strong> Nothing in the answer establishes a future vote.</li>
        <li><strong>B expressed doubt about remaining impartial - record.</strong> B said, "I do not know whether I could."</li>
        <li><strong>B must legally be removed - unknown.</strong> The judge would apply the jurisdiction's standard after the complete questioning and arguments.</li>
        <li><strong>C encountered an out-of-court confession claim - record.</strong> That describes exposure, not truth.</li>
        <li><strong>The confession claim is true - unknown.</strong> A social video is not admitted proof.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>4. Who decides?</h3>

      <p>The judge rules on a challenge for cause. A lawyer may request removal and the other side may respond, but the ruling belongs to the court.</p>

      <h3>5. A careful report</h3>

      <blockquote>Prospective jurors were questioned about relationships with law enforcement, personal experiences with robbery, and exposure to online claims; the court continued examining whether each person could decide the case from admitted evidence and the judge's instructions.</blockquote>

      <p>That sentence reports the subjects and the legal question. It does not announce bias, predict a vote, or turn an online claim into evidence.</p>
    </section>

